# The admin API. Binds 127.0.0.1 only — put Caddy or nginx in front of it for TLS. Binding
# 0.0.0.0 publishes a username/password login to the internet and nothing in the panel is
# designed for that.
#
# TWO THINGS ENFORCE THE VENDOR-KEY SEPARATION, AND THEY ARE INDEPENDENT ON PURPOSE.
#
#   1. EnvironmentFile= points at /etc/bayram/bayram-admin.env, a DIFFERENT file from the
#      bot's. AdminSettings has no field that could hold a vendor credential, and the
#      lifespan refuses to boot in prod if one is within reach — in the environment or in
#      that file.
#   2. InaccessiblePaths= on the bot's env file. The application-level refusal is a check
#      this process performs on itself; this one is the kernel's, and it survives a future
#      edit that points the unit at the wrong file. Belt and braces, because the whole
#      reason this is a third process is to keep the blast radius of a panel compromise
#      away from four credentials.
#
# Every operational action that needs a credential is an ARQ job bayram-worker performs.
#
# RECONCILED WITH THE INSTALLED UNIT, 2026-09-15. This file was a proposal and the machine
# had moved past it; two lines were wrong in a way that matters and are now what
# `systemctl cat bayram-admin.service` prints on the host.
#
#   * The environment reaches this process through systemd's EnvironmentFile=, NOT through
#     the BAYRAM_ADMIN_ENV_FILE selector this repository's documentation is built around.
#     That distinction is not cosmetic: the selector is what the boot log's `env_file` field
#     reports, so on this host that field names a file which is not the configuration
#     source and may not exist. Anyone debugging "the variable I set is not being read"
#     must edit /etc/bayram/bayram-admin.env and ignore the log line. See
#     docs/deployment/02-configuration.md, §"Precedence, and the one failure the error
#     message cannot describe".
#   * The interpreter is /opt/bayram/venv/bin/python, not a .venv under a /srv tree. The
#     /srv/bayram shape below is this repository's proposal and does not exist on the host.
#
# STILL UNRECONCILED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT GUESSED AT: WorkingDirectory= and the
# Documentation= URL below still carry the /srv/bayram proposal. WorkingDirectory is
# load-bearing — BAYRAM_ADMIN_DATA_ROOT defaults to the RELATIVE path `var`, so the panel
# and the worker agree about where archived media lives only if they share a working
# directory. Read it off the host with `systemctl show -p WorkingDirectory bayram-admin`
# before deploying this file anywhere; do not assume the line below.

[Unit]
Description=Bayram — admin API
Documentation=file:///srv/bayram/deploy/README.md
After=network-online.target postgresql.service
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=bayram
Group=bayram
WorkingDirectory=/srv/bayram
EnvironmentFile=/etc/bayram/bayram-admin.env
ExecStart=/opt/bayram/venv/bin/python -m uvicorn bayram.admin.app:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8080
Restart=always
RestartSec=5s
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

NoNewPrivileges=yes
PrivateTmp=yes
PrivateDevices=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
ProtectKernelTunables=yes
ProtectKernelModules=yes
ProtectControlGroups=yes
RestrictSUIDSGID=yes
RestrictRealtime=yes
RestrictNamespaces=yes
LockPersonality=yes
MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_INET AF_INET6 AF_UNIX
# Both spellings, because the guard is worthless if it names a file that no longer exists
# and systemd tolerates a listed path that is absent. The admin env file is verifiably
# `bayram-admin.env` on the host, so the bot's is almost certainly `bayram-bot.env` under
# the same convention — but "almost certainly" is not a basis for dropping the old name.
InaccessiblePaths=/etc/bayram/bayram-bot.env /etc/bayram/bot.env

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
